From dff92c1c72f5ffbc91010635567f1e5e61b61df6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lancethepants <lancethepants@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 20:04:15 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Planner: only compare channels that were scanned recently

A candidate with no neighbours in the scan cache scored 0.0, the best
possible score, even when nobody had looked at that channel: no data
looked like a quiet channel. On a router next to an AP on channel 36
the planner recommended moving to 36, and once a scan showed it busy,
the next never-visited block.

Even after a scan, the kernel keeps scan results for only about 30 s,
except for the BSSes heard on the operating channel, which it keeps
refreshing. Read as is, the current channel always had neighbours and
every other channel was empty, so the 2.4 GHz advice flipped between 6
and 11 each time it was followed. The survey cannot tell how recent a
visit is: mt76 overwrites a channel's active time with the dwell of the
last visit, so it does not grow, and its age is unknown.

Keep a memory of the scan cache: every BSS from 'scan dump' is kept for
15 minutes from when it was last heard. Follow 'iw event' for finished
scans (whoever started them: hostapd ACS, a site survey, 'iw scan') to
know which frequencies were visited and when; a channel in use always
counts as visited. A candidate is compared only if all its subchannels
were visited within the same 15 minutes. Otherwise it is marked
scanned=false, sorted last, shown with a '?' score and a 'not scanned in
the last 15 min' reason, and never recommended; a plan note says how
many candidates were skipped. Without survey data for the interface
this cannot be told and nothing changes. The demo source reports a scan
of every channel on each sample.
