index e804b9a..11c322f 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -413,6 +413,14 @@ it with `hostapd_cli help` before enabling control, and test on a lab AP first.
 - Channel candidates are the aligned blocks of the same width in the same band, from the phy's channel list (disabled
   channels excluded; DFS channels get a penalty because they need a channel-availability check). A switch is only
   recommended if it beats the current channel by a margin.
+- A candidate is only compared if a scan covered all its subchannels in the last 15 minutes. The daemon follows
+  `iw event` for finished scans (hostapd ACS, a site survey, `iw scan`), so it knows which channels were looked at;
+  others are shown as **not scanned** and never recommended, since no neighbours there means nobody looked, not
+  that the channel is quiet. Without survey data for the interface this cannot be told and every candidate counts
+  as scanned.
+- The kernel drops scan results after about 30 s but keeps refreshing the BSSes on the operating channel, so read
+  as is the current channel always looks busier than the others (and the advice flips back and forth). The daemon
+  keeps every BSS it has seen for 15 minutes instead. Run a scan, and the advice is good for the next 15 minutes.
 - Neighbours are counted on their **primary** channel only (scan results don't tell their width cheaply), and the AP
   does not scan on its own: the data is as old as the kernel's scan cache. The panel shows its age; use the
   "Trigger scan" button (control) or `hostapd` ACS to refresh it.
